<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndwandwe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ndwandwe Kingdom, Ndwandwe confederacy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ndwandwe were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> kingdom between the Pongola and Mfolozi rivers in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, the strongest power in the region in the 1810s under their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide</a>. They pressed the Swazi northwards and killed the Mthethwa ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a>, but were defeated by Shaka's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> around 1819. Their leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>, carried Ndwandwe traditions as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ndwandwe.jpg" alt="Ndwandwe" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SFC9394 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Africa_topo_continent.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
